nce Darcy velocity field and one example of the perturbed velocity field are shown in Figure 29 on a very coarse mesh for the purpose of illustration. We can see that even with a small amount of noise; the perturbed Darcy field is significantly chaotic; which makes the task of state estimation challenging. spatial tFilter on €Darcy","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":320.0,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":69.66666666666667,"r":526.0,"t":456.3333333333333},"charspan":[0,986],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/22","text":"mesh size h = 1/40 and on a very fine temporal mesh At = T/800 with T = 5. The data assimila tion algorithm is performed over the same mesh size and on a coarser temporal filtering steps tFilter T /NtFilter where N 2 50, with the same assumption the observational operator as in Test Case 2 Since concentration transport is driven by the Darcy velocity; we assume that there are some uncertainties in the process of solving the Darcy's flow system to increase the complexity of state estimation.  Thus, we add a small perturbation 0. OOO1ep to the reference Darcy velocities with N(O,I) and input the disturbed velocity to the forward solver: The reference Darcy velocity field and one example of the perturbed velocity field are shown in Figure 29 on a very coarse mesh for the purpose of illustration. We can see that even with a small amount of noise; the perturbed Darcy field is significantly chaotic; which makes the task of state estimation challenging. spatial tFilter on €Darcy"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Similar to Test Case 2, we aim to investigate the performance of the United Filter under var ious levels of perturbation. Thus, we consider two types of disturbed noise 0 in (14): õ1 0.001vA ẽl and õ2 = where ẽi 1,2. Regarding the setting for the parameter estimation; as the exact values are small, we apply the assimilation algorithm to approximate pi = 1/di,i = 1,2 and pr = 1/ar We choose the number of Direct Filter particles to be M = 40, and the number of iterations in the United Filter to be R = 4tFilter '","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":240.33333333333337,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":69.66666666666667,"r":525.3333333333334,"t":323.0},"charspan":[0,515],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/23","text":"Similar to Test Case 2, we aim to investigate the performance of the United Filter under var ious levels of perturbation. Thus, we consider two types of disturbed noise 0 in (14): õ1 0.001vA ẽl and õ2 = where ẽi 1,2. Regarding the setting for the parameter estimation; as the exact values are small, we apply the assimilation algorithm to approximate pi = 1/di,i = 1,2 and pr = 1/ar We choose the number of Direct Filter particles to be M = 40, and the number of iterations in the United Filter to be R = 4tFilter '"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"caption","orig":"Figure 31: [Test Case 3] Heat map of estimated concentration field by the United Filter: (Left) With noise õ1 . (Right) With noise õ2 .","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/2"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":74.0,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.0,"r":525.3333333333334,"t":104.0},"charspan":[0,135],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/24","text":"Figure 31: [Test Case 3] Heat map of estimated concentration field by the United Filter: (Left) With noise õ1 . (Right) With noise õ2 ."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"0.8","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/2"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":207.66666666666663,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":139.66666666666666,"r":148.33333333333334,"t":213.0},"charspan":[0,3],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/25","text":"0.8"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"2.5","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/2"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":209.33333333333337,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":265.3333333333333,"r":277.3333333333333,"t":217.33333333333337},"charspan":[0,3],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/26","text":"2.5"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"b